diagnosis_text:
A thorough inspection of the entire dc_clos_bgp fabric reveals no fault. Every component that could explain the reported timeout was examined and found healthy. Both leaf routers (leaf_router_0_0, leaf_router_0_1) have all interfaces UP with correct IP assignments and zero packet errors or drops. All seven eBGP peering sessions across the fabric — leaf-to-spine (4 sessions), spine-to-super-spine (2 sessions) — are in Established state, have been up for over two minutes, and are exchanging prefixes correctly. Both leaf routers carry a valid FIB entry for the remote /24: leaf_router_0_0 has 10.0.1.0/24 via spine_router_0_1, and leaf_router_0_1 has 10.0.0.0/24 via spine_router_0_1. Both pc_0_0 and pc_0_1 have the correct IP addresses and point to their respective leaf routers as their default gateway. No iptables rules exist on any forwarding device. Bidirectional ping between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) completes with 0% packet loss in both directions. The network is operating normally. The maintenance window referenced in the ticket either involved a transient disruption (e.g., a brief link flap or router restart) from which the fabric has fully converged and self-healed, or the report was filed while BGP was still in its initial convergence phase after the lab was brought up. No corrective action is required; the user should retry their application and confirm connectivity is restored.
=== END ===